title: Animation principles for interfaces
lang: en
publishedAt: 2026-08-22
draft: false
summary: "Try animation principles on familiar controls, from saving a draft to opening a sort menu."
cover: '/images/writing/twelve-principles-one-square.png'
---
When I open a sort menu, I usually know what I want. I’m looking for Recent so I can get back to the file I was working on. If I have to wait for the menu to settle before I can spot it, I feel that pause every time.

That’s how I like to judge animation in an interface. Here are nine of the twelve animation principles, tried on things you’d actually click. [Raphael Salaja’s guide](https://www.raphaelsalaja.com/library/12-principles-of-animation) explains where the principles come from.

#### Squash and stretch

## Let the button give a little

{% ui-demo kind="squash" /%}

Press and hold each Save button. In **Whole button**, the words get squashed too. In **Surface only**, the button gives a little under your finger and the label stays readable.

#### Anticipation

## Show what’s about to happen

{% ui-demo kind="anticipation" /%}

Say you’re about to archive a brief. In **Before the click**, you get a hint about where it will go. Hover over Archive or tab to it to see the hint. When you click, the brief moves right away.

#### Follow through and overlapping action

## Let a short list arrive in order

{% ui-demo kind="stagger" /%}

Open the alerts and watch the rows that arrive **60ms apart**. Three are easy to follow. Give twenty rows the same gap and the last one starts more than a second later. For a long list, I’d bring them in together.

#### Slow in and slow out

## Let a panel slow down as it lands

{% ui-demo kind="easing" /%}

Both details panels take the same time to open. The **Ease-out** panel does most of its moving early and slows down at the end. It feels quick because you see it answer your click almost right away.

#### Arcs

## Show where the file went

{% ui-demo kind="arc" /%}

Pin the brief and watch where it goes. The curved path takes it across first, then up to Pinned. A small bounce marks the moment it lands.

#### Timing

## Try a menu you open all day

{% ui-demo kind="duration" /%}

Open both sort menus and pick Recent. The **180ms** menu settles sooner, so you can see what you’re reaching for earlier. That little difference matters when you’re sorting files over and over. Emil Kowalski makes the same point about how often people see an animation. [You Don’t Need Animations](https://emilkowal.ski/ui/you-dont-need-animations)

#### Exaggeration

## Give an error one clear nudge

{% ui-demo kind="shake" /%}

Try verifying the code before you type anything. Both forms tell you what to enter. The shake makes the message easier to catch when your eye is already moving on.

#### Solid drawing

## Use a shadow when something floats

{% ui-demo kind="depth" /%}

Open Sort and look where the menu crosses the file rows. A small shadow helps you see that the menu is sitting above the list and you’ll be back at the files when it closes.

#### Appeal

## Save a small flourish for a small moment

{% ui-demo kind="appeal" /%}

When I’m going through a folder, I might mark ten files I want to come back to. I want each heart to fill so I can move on to the next one. When I save one thing I really like, the little hop feels right.
